  "subtitle": "The first thumb-operated trackball, the input grammar laptops would later standardize",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum's trackballs are 3D robots (Radio Drum) and game controllers — none is the desktop thumb trackball whose operator grammar every laptop later borrowed. The Logitech TrackMan (1989) was the first to put a large stationary ball under the thumb, freeing the palm and fingers to rest, a mirror of the finger-operated trackball design that came before it. This inversion of who works the ball is the entire interaction: a pointing device that asks your thumb to do what the whole hand used to do, and the direct ancestor of a decade of portable-computer pointing.",
